---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { blogPostingJsonLd } from "../../lib/seo";

const h1 = "Banned on Christmas Eve. Here's how to fight a Facebook ban.";
const description =
  "Hacked, banned, and stuck in Meta's appeal maze? Read the Indie Doc Journey Special Dispatch: Christmas Eve ban, smoking gun, and the small-claims win.";
const path = "/special-dispatch/facebook-banned-on-christmas-eve";

/** Magazine PDF download only (not the HTML body). */
const pdfHref =
  "/special-dispatch/Banned-on-Christmas-Eve-Part-1-Magazine.pdf";

/** Existing Journey companion for Part 2. Do not invent a Part 2 Astro page. */
const part2Href =
  "/indie-doc-journey/i-sued-meta-in-small-claims-court-and-won/";
const part2Exists = true;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Banned on Christmas Eve"
  fullTitle="Banned on Christmas Eve | Facebook Ban Survival + Meta Small Claims"
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={blogPostingJsonLd({ title: h1, description, url: path })}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="section__label">
      <a href="/special-dispatch/" style="text-decoration: none;"
        >← Special Dispatch</a
      >
      {" · "}
      <a href="/indie-doc-journey" style="text-decoration: none;">Journey</a>
    </p>

    <p class="section__label plate--subtitle">
      Indie Doc Journey · Special Dispatch № 01
    </p>

    <h1>{h1}</h1>

    <p>
      Christmas Eve, Meta permanently disabled a 22-year account for the crime of
      getting hacked. This Special Dispatch is the survival map: the appeal
      abyss, the smoking gun in Meta's own logs, and the small-claims fight that
      got the account back. If you are a Facebook exile, start here.
    </p>

    <div class="hero__ctas" style="margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href={pdfHref} download
        >Download: Banned on Christmas Eve (PDF)</a
      >
      {
        part2Exists && (
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={part2Href}
            >Part 2 on Journey (existing)</a
          >
        )
      }
    </div>

    <h2>Start here</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Part 1 (this page):</strong> the ban night playbook below
      </li>
      {
        part2Exists ? (
          <li>
            <a href={part2Href}>Part 2: I sued Meta and won</a>
            {" "}
            (existing Journey post)
          </li>
        ) : (
          <li>Part 2: coming on Journey</li>
        )
      }
      <li>
        <a href={pdfHref}>Download: Banned on Christmas Eve (PDF)</a>
        {" "}
        magazine export for offline reading
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The digital death sentence</h2>
    <p>
      Christmas Eve. Instead of cocoa, a disable notice: a 22-year Facebook
      account, permanently gone. No warning. No explanation. Just cold absolute
      language that assumes you already know what you did wrong, and that nobody
      is coming to help.
    </p>
    <p>
      That account held family photos, milestones, grief, jokes, and two decades
      of digital life. Losing it on a holiday, when support is a ghost town, is
      the digital death sentence. If you have been there, you already know the
      dopamine withdrawal is real.
    </p>

    <h2>Into the appeal abyss</h2>
    <p>
      Meta's appeal process assumes guilt first. The forms ask you to explain
      what you did wrong. The drop-downs do not include "I got hacked." Waiting
      stretches from days to months. Automated replies arrive on schedule and say
      almost nothing useful.
    </p>
    <p>
      Meta Verified mostly buys a shinier brush-off: elevated "human" support that
      still runs you in circles. The maze is designed to exhaust you into
      acceptance. Stubborn documentation beats spammy re-appeals.
    </p>

    <h2>The smoking gun in Meta's own logs</h2>
    <p>
      The breakthrough lived in Meta's own plumbing. Trying
      {" "}
      <a href="https://www.facebook.com/hacked" rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >hacked.facebook.com</a
      >
      {" "}
      can surface a checkpoint URL that includes language like
      <code>login_with_hacked_cookie</code>. That is cookie theft: a session
      breach that never needs your password, so two-factor never fires.
    </p>
    <p>
      When Facebook's own system admits the compromise and still treats you like
      the criminal, you have receipts. Keep the screenshots, the odd URLs, the
      timestamps. That smoking gun carried this fight into small claims.
    </p>

    <h2>Essential steps if Facebook banned you</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Act fast.</strong> You generally have about 180 days to appeal
        before a suspension hardens into permanence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Document everything.</strong> Screenshot every error, email, and
        URL, especially anything with "hacked" or "disabled" in it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Download your data</strong> immediately if you still can
        (Settings → Your Facebook Information → Download Your Information).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Try the hacked-account flow</strong> at hacked.facebook.com and
        look for session or cookie-theft clues.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Don't spam appeals.</strong> One calm, well-written appeal beats
        a dozen angry ones.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Meta Verified is a last resort.</strong> It sometimes escalates
        support. Results vary wildly. Treat it as optional, not magic.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>What if Facebook banned me?</h3>
    <p>
      Start with this Part 1 playbook: what the disable notice looks like, how
      the appeal abyss works, and how to keep your sanity while you hunt for
      proof. Grab the
      {" "}
      <a href={pdfHref}>magazine PDF</a>
      {" "}
      if you want the offline cut.
    </p>

    <h3>Hacked, then banned?</h3>
    <p>
      That is the trap. Cookie theft can look like "you" from Meta's side, then
      the platform disables the account and gaslights the appeal. This page
      covers the smoking gun in Meta's own logs.
      {
        part2Exists && (
          <>
            {" "}
            The courtroom chapter lives on Journey as
            {" "}
            <a href={part2Href}>Part 2: I sued Meta and won</a>.
          </>
        )
      }
    </p>

    <h3>Can you sue Meta in small claims?</h3>
    <p>
      {
        part2Exists ? (
          <>
            Yes. The small-claims win that got the account back is documented on
            Journey:
            {" "}
            <a href={part2Href}
              >Part 2: I sued Meta in small claims court and won</a
            >. This hub ships Part 1 first; Part 2 stays on Journey for now.
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            The small-claims chapter is coming on Journey. This page ships the
            Part 1 survival map first.
          </>
        )
      }
    </p>

    <h2>Series path</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>This hub + Part 1</strong> (you are here):
        {" "}
        <a href="/special-dispatch/facebook-banned-on-christmas-eve/"
          >Special Dispatch № 01</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={pdfHref}>Download the Part 1 magazine PDF</a>
      </li>
      {
        part2Exists ? (
          <li>
            <a href={part2Href}>Part 2 on Journey</a>
            {" "}
            (existing post; Astro Part 2 waits)
          </li>
        ) : (
          <li>Part 2: coming on Journey</li>
        )
      }
      <li>
        <a href="/special-dispatch/facebook-banned-on-christmas-eve/"
          >Back here</a
        >
        {" "}
        when you need the map again
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href={pdfHref} download>Download PDF</a>
      {" "}
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/special-dispatch/">All Special Dispatches</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
